#!/bin/execlineb -P
# Clear every rfkill SOFT block at boot, before NetworkManager starts (its
# dependencies.d/river-rfkill), on the live medium and on the installed node.
#
# Nothing else in the image ever touches rfkill. A radio that the firmware or a previous OS
# left soft-blocked stays blocked across a reboot, and the kill switch survives into Linux:
# the card enumerates, iwlwifi binds it and the firmware loads, so `ip link` shows the
# interface -- but NetworkManager reports the device unavailable and `nmcli device wifi list`
# returns nothing at all. That is indistinguishable, from the console, from a missing driver,
# and it is why the AX201 in the owner's XPS 13 9300 looked dead on a medium whose driver,
# firmware and PCI alias were all verified present (2026-10-05).
#
# A HARD block is a physical switch and is deliberately NOT touched: `rfkill unblock` cannot
# clear one and must not pretend to.
#
# Never fails the boot -- NetworkManager depends on this oneshot, so a non-zero exit here
# would keep the network down, which is the very failure it exists to prevent.
foreground { /usr/bin/rfkill unblock all }
exit 0
